      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Two patients with severe angina pectoris during minimal physical exercise and angiologically proven severe coronary stenosis also had a surgically operable carcinoma of the bronchus and stomach, respectively. Extensive operation for carcinoma would have been a serious cardiological risk. A preceding aortocoronary bypass operation would have been an additional stress as well as a delay of surgery by several weeks. Using percutaneous transluminal coronary dilatation it was possible to remove severe and isolated coronary stenoses rapidly and with little effort. Both survived carcinoma operations a few days later without trouble."},
